CHAPTER ONE — INTRODUCTION
1.1 Background
The proliferation of mobile devices and the central role of digital resources in learning have made robust campus wireless networks essential. Traditional flat network designs often fail as user density and multimedia demands increase, causing performance degradation and security gaps. This project proposes and validates a controller‑based campus WLAN architecture with VLAN segmentation, WPA2‑Enterprise (802.1X) authentication backed by RADIUS, QoS for real‑time traffic, and redundancy for high availability.

1.2 Problem statement
The university’s wireless network is susceptible to unauthorized access, lacks segmentation for research/administration, and cannot guarantee quality for VoIP and video streaming.

1.3 Objectives
- Design a VLAN-segmented campus network supporting 12 department/area VLANs and dedicated WLAN SSIDs.
- Implement centralized authentication using WPA2‑Enterprise and RADIUS.
- Configure QoS policies to prioritize VoIP (DSCP EF) and video streaming (AF41).
- Validate roaming, authentication latency, VLAN isolation and QoS via Packet Tracer simulation and document results.
- Produce a deployment-ready configuration bundle and a full report.

1.4 Scope & limitations
Scope: IPv4-only design, Packet Tracer simulation, sample RADIUS integration (FreeRADIUS/NPS).
Limitations: RF behavior, real-world interference, and some enterprise WLC HA behaviors are not fully modeled in Packet Tracer. A real site survey is recommended before deployment.

CHAPTER TWO — LITERATURE REVIEW
2.1 Introduction
This chapter surveys campus WLAN architectures, 802.1X/RADIUS authentication, and QoS mechanisms for VoIP and video. It establishes best practices that inform the design.

2.2 Review of Similar Network Systems
- Flat VLAN-only designs: simple to deploy but suffer scalability and security issues.
- Controller-based architectures (WLC + CAPWAP): centralized management, easier SSID/policy rollout, better roaming support.
- 802.1X & RADIUS: per-user authentication widely recommended in campus environments to enforce credentials and policy.
- QoS models: DiffServ with DSCP EF for voice and AF for video is standard.

Figure 2.1 — Comparative Architecture
(Insert file: 02-Comparative-Architecture.svg)
Caption: shows advantages (central auth, policy, roaming).

2.3 Summary and Gap Analysis
Most literature supports controller-based designs for medium–large campuses and recommends segmentation and 802.1X. Gaps: many works do not address guest captive portal integration and research lab isolation simultaneously—this project addresses both.

