CHAPTER THREE — RESEARCH METHODOLOGY (SIMULATION DESIGN)
3.1 Introduction
Design Science methodology: build an artifact (network design) and evaluate via simulation in Packet Tracer.

3.2 Justification for Simulation & Simulator
3.2.1 Why simulation?
- Simulation enables controlled evaluation of policies (802.1X flows, roaming) with lower cost than physical deployment.
3.2.2 Why Cisco Packet Tracer?
- Packet Tracer supports topology building, WLC/AP behaviors, server simulation (DHCP, HTTP, RADIUS) and is suitable for teaching and final year projects. Limitations: RF fidelity, advanced WLC HA behaviors may be limited.
3.2.3 Simulation Flowchart and Logical Diagram
- Insert flowchart: 03-Simulation-Flowchart.svg
- Insert logical topology screenshot: 03-Logical-Topology.svg
- Caption each figure and include short method notes: VLAN mapping, device IPs, and management VLAN.

3.3 Simulation Data Definition
3.3.1 Measurement metrics
- Throughput (kbps), RTT (ms), Jitter (ms), Packet loss (%), Authentication time (ms), Reassociation latency (ms).
3.3.2 Simulation Configuration Table
- Table of parameters: number of clients, packet sizes, traffic patterns (RTP for voice, TCP for HTTP/video), number of concurrent sessions.
3.3.3 Measurement collection strategy
- Use Packet Tracer CLI logs, server logs, and manual timestamping to produce CSV files for plotting.
3.3.4 Simulation Results (graphs)
- Export graphs from Excel based on test outputs: Graph-Throughput.png, Graph-RTT.png, Graph-Jitter.png.

3.4 Ethical considerations and validity
Simulated user accounts used for testing; no private data.

CHAPTER FOUR — PRESENTATION OF RESULTS AND ANALYSIS
4.1 Introduction
Summarize the test plan and present results in chronological order.

4.2 Presentation of Results and Comparative Analysis
Authentication: show RADIUS Access-Accept logs screenshot (Auth-Logs-Access-Accept.png). Report authentication success rates and average authentication latency.
VLAN isolation: ping tests showing successful Staff→Server and failed Guest→Server (Ping-Results-VLAN-Isolation.png).
QoS: voice traffic priority—show sample pcap/pdu logs or Packet Tracer simulation traces indicating prioritized RTP packets (Simulation-PDU-Event-1.png).
Throughput & latency graphs: include Graph-Throughput.png and Graph-RTT.png. Present tables with averages and standard deviations.

4.3 Comparative Analysis
Compare measured results to expectations and literature (e.g., acceptable VoIP jitter < 30 ms; average RTT for voice < 50 ms). Discuss how controller-based design improved roaming vs flat designs.

4.4 Summary & Threats to validity
Discuss Packet Tracer limits and suggest real-world site survey.

CHAPTER FIVE — CONCLUSIONS AND RECOMMENDATIONS
5.1 Summary of Findings
Design met objectives: segmentation, secure auth, guest isolation, voice/video QoS, and redundancy configured in design.

5.2 Conclusions
A controller-based campus WLAN with WPA2‑Enterprise and adequate VLAN design is suitable for campus needs; Packet Tracer simulation confirms functional flows.

5.3 Recommendations
- Conduct on-site RF survey before deployment.
- Use AD integration (NPS) in production for centralized auth.
- Consider WPA3-Enterprise in future for improved security.
- Schedule redundancy tests and periodic audits.

5.4 Future Work
Extend tests to include realistic RF interference studies, real hardware pilot, and IPv6 addressing.

